(3) Map projections compromise between shapes, areas, distances and directions. The Mercator conformal cylindrical preserves angles but inflates a meridian's scale far from the equator, so a rhumb line renders straight; the transverse variant rotates the cylinder ninety degrees, concentrating distortion in meridian zones, and the universal transverse mercator slices the globe into sixty zones. Datum transformations convert between reference ellipsoids, whose parameters differ in the flattening ratio and equatorial radius; seven-parameter similarity transforms rotate, scale and translate between Cartesian coordinates, and the geodetic conversion orders the axes in east, north, up. Leveling measures gravity potential differences rather than raw ellipsoidal heights, and a geoid model converts between the two; similarly the vertical datum moves if global sea level changes are added to regional isostatic contrasts. Precision requirements set the geometry: a nine-decimal longitude resolves to one micron, far beyond survey accuracy, so round trip degrees are published at 0.000001 wisdom digits.

